   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Dear all,</div><div><br></div><div>I forgot how site covariates must be formatted for the detection model in a function like spPGOcc()? Do I have to clone them until they have the right (matrix) dimension to be an observational covariate ?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks and best regards  --- Marc<br></div></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "i9gAu4iiDgAJ",
   "from": "José Wagner Ribeiro Júnior",
   "author": "5df889d339ff56a5",
   "date": "2025-01-27T18:25:47Z",
   "subject": "Re: format of site covariates in the detection model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif;font-size:large\">Hi Marc, </div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif;font-size:large\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif;font-size:large\">It’s not necessary to clone them as the detection matrix. For site-level covariates in the det.covs object, it should be a one-dimensional vector. The det.covs object is a list where each element corresponds to a covariate to include in the detection portion of the model.</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif;font-size:large\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:verdana,sans-serif;font-size:large\">Best regards,<br>José</div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Mon, Jan 27, 2025 at 2:39 PM Marc Kéry &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ahm.e...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
